# TypeScript Standards

## Prefer Explicit Types for Public APIs
Use explicit return types and parameter types for exported functions and public methods.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - inferred, unclear contract
export function parseUser(input) {
  return JSON.parse(input);
}

// ✅ GOOD - explicit types
export function parseUser(input: string): User {
  return JSON.parse(input) as User;
}
```

## Avoid `any`
Use `unknown` when the type is truly unknown, or narrow with type guards.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function handle(data: any) {
  return data.value;
}

// ✅ GOOD
function handle(data: unknown): string {
  if (typeof data === 'object' && data !== null && 'value' in data) {
    return String((data as { value: unknown }).value);
  }
  throw new Error('Invalid data');
}
```

## Use `const` Assertions for Literals
When you need a value to be treated as a literal type:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const status = "pending";

// ✅ GOOD - when you need the literal type
const status = "pending" as const;
```
